aws/ACM|domain|checked: acm/2015-12-08 mutates a certificate only through UpdateCertificateOptions (transparency logging), RenewCertificate, ResendValidationEmail and tags — the domain is an input to RequestCertificate and no operation changes it
aws/ACM|validation.method|checked: same operation list — the validation method is chosen at RequestCertificate and no operation changes it on an issued certificate
aws/Aurora|encryption.atRest|checked: ModifyDBCluster's only KMS member is MasterUserSecretKmsKeyId, the key for the MANAGED MASTER-USER SECRET — storage encryption is not among its members (botocore rds/2014-10-31)
aws/Aurora|encryption.customerManagedKeys|checked: same evidence — ModifyDBCluster cannot change the cluster's storage key
aws/Bedrock|inference.destinationRegions|checked: same evidence — the routing set is part of the profile and there is no operation that updates one
aws/Bedrock|model.provider|checked: bedrock/2023-04-20 has CreateInferenceProfile, DeleteInferenceProfile, GetInferenceProfile and ListInferenceProfiles and NO Update — a profile's model source cannot be changed
aws/EBSVolume|availability.class|checked: no Modify*/Update*/Put*/Set* operation on a volume in the ec2 model accepts an AvailabilityZone — a volume lives in the zone it was created in
aws/EBSVolume|encryption.atRest|checked: ModifyVolume accepts Size, VolumeType, Iops, Throughput and MultiAttachEnabled — no encryption and no key (botocore ec2/2016-11-15)
aws/EBSVolume|encryption.customerManagedKeys|checked: same evidence — a volume's key cannot be changed, only a re-encrypted copy created
aws/EC2Instance|availability.class|checked: no Modify*/Update*/Put*/Set* operation on an instance in the ec2 model accepts a Placement or AvailabilityZone — an instance cannot move zones
aws/ECR|encryption.customerManagedKeys|checked: encryptionConfiguration is accepted only by CreateRepository (the two other operations carrying it configure creation TEMPLATES) — there is no update path for a repository's key (botocore ecr/2015-09-21)
aws/EKSAddon|addon.name|checked: the providerId ENCODES the addon name (eks-addon:<region>:<cluster>/<addon>), so changing it addresses a different addon — the same argument that settles a resource whose identity is in its name
aws/EKSPodIdentity|workload.namespace|checked: the providerId ENCODES this value (eks-podidentity:<region>:<cluster>/<namespace>/<serviceAccount>), so changing it addresses a different association — the same argument that settles a resource whose location is part of its name
aws/EKSPodIdentity|workload.serviceAccount|checked: the providerId ENCODES this value (eks-podidentity:<region>:<cluster>/<namespace>/<serviceAccount>), so changing it addresses a different association — the same argument that settles a resource whose location is part of its name
aws/ElastiCacheServerless|encryption.customerManagedKeys|checked: ModifyServerlessCache accepts Description, CacheUsageLimits, user groups, security groups, snapshot settings, Engine and MajorEngineVersion — and NO KMS key, so the key really is set at create (botocore elasticache/2015-02-02)
aws/LB|network.publicExposure|checked: elbv2 has SetSubnets, SetSecurityGroups and SetIpAddressType but NO operation that sets Scheme — an internet-facing/internal load balancer cannot be flipped (botocore elbv2/2015-12-01)
aws/OpenSearchServerless|encryption.customerManagedKeys|checked: UpdateSecurityPolicy DOES edit an encryption policy, and AWS's own documentation says "You can't change the encryption key for a collection after the collection is created" and that modifying a policy's rules "doesn't affect existing collections that have KMS keys already assigned" — the same mechanism as the network policy, opposite semantics
aws/RDS|engine.protocol|checked: ModifyDBInstance DOES carry an Engine member, and its own documentation scopes it to converting an Oracle non-CDB to a CDB (oracle-ee-cdb / oracle-se2-cdb) — there is no operation that changes the engine FAMILY (botocore rds/2014-10-31)
aws/Route53Record|dns.type|checked: the providerId ENCODES the record type (r53rec:<zoneID>:<recordType>:<name>), and a DNS record is keyed by (name, type) — a different type is a different record
aws/S3|retention.locked|checked: S3 Object Lock is enabled by a header on CreateBucket and cannot be turned off afterwards (botocore s3/2006-03-01 has no operation that disables it)
aws/SQS|delivery.guarantee|checked: SetQueueAttributes' own documentation enumerates the attributes it uses and FifoQueue is NOT among them (ContentBasedDeduplication, DeduplicationScope and FifoThroughputLimit are) — the QueueAttributeName enum is shared with GetQueueAttributes and proves nothing on its own
aws/SQS|ordering.enabled|checked: same evidence — FIFO is chosen by the queue's .fifo name at CreateQueue and SetQueueAttributes cannot set FifoQueue
azure/ACR|encryption.customerManagedKeys|checked: Microsoft's own documentation says "You can currently enable a customer-managed key only while creating a registry" and "You can't disable the encryption after you enable a customer-managed key" — the ARM model lists encryption under RegistryPropertiesUpdateParameters, and the SERVICE is the authority over the wire shape
