gcp/AR|encryption.customerManagedKeys|checked: Google's own field description ends "This value may not be changed after the Repository has been created" (artifactregistry/v1 Repository.kmsKeyName)
gcp/AR|location.region|checked: the driver addresses the resource at /projects/{p}/locations/{region}/repositories/{id} — the location is part of the resource name, so a patch at another one addresses a different resource
gcp/BackupPlanGCP|location.region|checked: the driver addresses the resource at /projects/{p}/locations/{region}/backupPlans/{id} — the location is part of the resource name, so a patch at another one addresses a different resource
gcp/CloudDNSRecord|dns.type|checked: the providerId ENCODES the record type (gdnsrec:<project>:<zone>:<recordType>:<name>), and a DNS record is keyed by (name, type) — a different type is a different record
gcp/CloudSQL|engine.protocol|checked: the D827 sweep of sqladmin/v1 found only failover, switchover, promoteReplica and the restore family — no method converts an instance between engine families
gcp/CloudSQL|location.region|checked: D827 swept sqladmin/v1 and found no method that moves an instance between regions — only failover, switchover, promoteReplica and the restore family, none of which changes where the instance lives
gcp/GCEInstance|availability.class|checked: an instance is addressed at /projects/{p}/zones/{zone}/instances/{i} — the zone is part of the resource name, so a change addresses a different machine
gcp/GCEInstance|location.region|checked: the driver addresses the resource at /projects/{p}/zones/{zone}/instances/{id} — the location is part of the resource name, so a patch at another one addresses a different resource
gcp/GKEAddon|addon.name|checked: the providerId ENCODES the addon name (gke-addon:<project>:<location>:<cluster>:<addon>), so changing it addresses a different addon — the same argument that settles a resource whose identity is in its name
gcp/GKEWorkloadIdentity|workload.namespace|checked: the providerId ENCODES this value (gke-wi:<poolProject>:<gsaEmail>:<namespace>:<serviceAccount>), so changing it addresses a different association — the same argument that settles a resource whose location is part of its name
gcp/GKEWorkloadIdentity|workload.serviceAccount|checked: the providerId ENCODES this value (gke-wi:<poolProject>:<gsaEmail>:<namespace>:<serviceAccount>), so changing it addresses a different association — the same argument that settles a resource whose location is part of its name
gcp/GKE|availability.class|checked: a GKE cluster's location IS its topology (a zone for zonal, a region for regional) and the container/v1 API has no method that changes it
gcp/GKE|location.region|checked: the driver addresses the resource at /projects/{p}/locations/{region}/clusters/{id} — the location is part of the resource name, so a patch at another one addresses a different resource
gcp/LoadBalancer|encryption.inTransit|checked: the posture selects an HTTP vs an HTTPS target proxy, and forwardingRules.setTarget says in Google's own discovery document that "the new target should be of the same type as the old target" — so the proxy cannot be swapped under the rule
gcp/LoadBalancer|network.publicExposure|checked: forwardingRules.patch says in Google's own discovery document that only the network_tier field can be patched, so loadBalancingScheme is create-time
gcp/LogBucket|location.region|checked: the driver addresses the resource at /projects/{p}/locations/{region}/buckets/{id} — the location is part of the resource name, so a patch at another one addresses a different resource
gcp/MIG|availability.class|checked: zonal and regional managed instance groups are DIFFERENT API resources (instanceGroupManagers vs regionInstanceGroupManagers in the compute/v1 discovery document), so there is no setting to change
gcp/MIG|location.region|checked: the driver addresses the resource at /projects/{p}/regions/{region}/instanceGroupManagers/{id} — the location is part of the resource name, so a patch at another one addresses a different resource
gcp/PD|availability.class|checked: zonal and regional disks are DIFFERENT resources in compute/v1 (disks vs regionDisks), and a zonal disk is addressed at /projects/{p}/zones/{zone}/disks/{d} — the class is part of the identity
gcp/PD|encryption.customerManagedKeys|checked: compute/v1 offers disks.updateKmsKey, but its own description limits it to rotating "to the latest version" of the same key, and no method attaches a key to a disk created without one — the boolean this attribute carries is create-time
gcp/PD|location.region|checked: the driver addresses the resource at /projects/{p}/regions/{region}/disks/{id} — the location is part of the resource name, so a patch at another one addresses a different resource
gcp/PubSubQueue|ordering.enabled|checked: `gcloud pubsub subscriptions update` builds an explicit update mask, and its synopsis carries --enable-exactly-once-delivery but NO ordering flag — message ordering is chosen at create
gcp/Secret|encryption.customerManagedKeys|checked: the driver puts the key in replication.userManaged.replicas[].customerManagedEncryption, and Google's own discovery document says of Secret.replication "Immutable. The replication policy ... cannot be changed after the Secret has been created"
gcp/Secret|location.region|checked: the provider says it in the schema — Secret.replication is documented "Optional. Immutable. The replication policy cannot be changed after the Secret has been created", and the replication policy is where a secret's location lives
gcp/Vertex|inference.destinationRegions|checked: the destination region follows the endpoint's location, which is part of the resource name (/projects/{p}/locations/{region}/endpoints/{id}) — the same argument that settles Vertex's location.region
gcp/Vertex|location.region|checked: the driver addresses the resource at /projects/{p}/locations/{region}/endpoints/{id} — the location is part of the resource name, so a patch at another one addresses a different resource
